= Conclusion

This project investigated the validity and quality of CT adequacy calculations generated by SECP Global for the planning-phase engineering use case. The assessment combined numerical verification against an independent reference calculation with an examination of traceability, functional coverage and report quality. Three representative application–device cases were investigated: transformer differential protection with RET670, line differential protection with RED670 and overcurrent protection requiring REF650.

The results show that the quality of the investigated functions differs significantly between protection applications. For line differential protection, the numerical results produced by SECP showed very good agreement with the independent reference calculation. The remaining deviations were small, conservative and did not affect any suitability decision within the evaluated dataset. The implemented calculation approach was therefore found to be technically appropriate for the investigated cases. In contrast, the transformer differential implementation contains a systematic defect caused by the double consideration of the secondary lead resistance. Although the resulting suitability decisions remained unchanged in all evaluated cases, the reported required values are numerically incorrect and may result in unnecessarily conservative CT dimensioning. The overcurrent assessment revealed a different limitation. The required REF650 device is currently not available within SECP and the equivalence of the offered REF630 substitute could not be demonstrated. Consequently, the intended engineering assessment cannot presently be performed for this use case.

One of the most important findings of this work is that the final suitability decision alone is not sufficient to validate an engineering calculation tool. All twenty numerically comparable cases resulted in identical pass/fail decisions between SECP and the independent reference calculation, despite the presence of a systematic calculation defect in the transformer differential implementation. Had the evaluation been limited to decision-level comparison, this defect would not have been detected. The results therefore demonstrate the importance of validating intermediate values, calculation formulae and underlying engineering assumptions rather than focusing solely on the final verdict.

The assessment also showed that releasability depends on more than numerical correctness. While the generated reports contain the required engineering information and support traceability in principle, substantial deficiencies were identified in formatting, maintainability and document quality. The extensive use of formula fields, poor page layout, inconsistent table formatting, visual artefacts and references to superseded standards reduce the usability of the reports as customer-facing engineering deliverables. These findings affect all investigated applications and cannot be attributed to a single calculation function. They therefore represent a general limitation of the current report-generation process.

Overall, the results do not support an unrestricted release of SECP-generated CT calculation reports for the investigated planning-phase use case. Nevertheless, the study also demonstrates that the tool already provides technically reliable results for specific applications, most notably line differential protection. Future improvements should focus on correcting the transformer differential calculation, addressing the overcurrent coverage gap and improving the quality of the generated reports. Beyond these specific improvements, the work highlights the value of independent reference calculations and structured validation procedures as effective means of identifying engineering defects that may otherwise remain hidden behind apparently correct final decisions.